Adaptive Measurement Validity: 

A Formal Framework for Construct-Sufficient Benchmark Generation

ouadi Maakoul 

AI: chatGpt, Deepseek, Meta AI, Claude,Grok XAi 

Abstract

We develop a formal framework for evaluating the validity of adaptive benchmarks—benchmarks whose construction process depends on the data they are intended to measure. We formalize the problem of Adaptive Measurement Invariance (AMI): under what conditions does regeneration of a benchmark preserve the construct it was designed to measure? We introduce the construct-sufficient representation  T_C^\star  as the minimal transformation of a data distribution that preserves all information necessary to measure a target construct  C . We replace the non-transitive Blackwell sufficiency order with a decision-theoretic equivalence class that is transitive and directly testable. We propose a falsifiable invariance-testing protocol combining stratified extraction fidelity, ordinal IRT diagnostics with explicit assumption-checking, alignment optimization for small-sample invariance testing, and equivalence tests with margins derived from the nomological network. We extend the framework to Information-Adaptive Computation (IAC), reformulating metareasoning as the acquisition of construct-sufficient information under cost constraints, and we identify the additional assumptions required for a linear value-of-computation relation. This is a theoretical contribution: no empirical data are collected, no simulations are run, and no models are trained. The framework's formal results are proven deductively; its protocols are proposed and remain to be validated empirically. We provide an explicit audit distinguishing proven results, definitional choices, borrowed theorems, conjectures, and speculative extensions.

---

1. Introduction

Benchmarks are measurement instruments. When a benchmark is adaptive—when its construction process depends on the data it measures—the instrument changes each time it is applied. The crucial question is whether it continues to measure the same construct.

Recent work on construct validity in LLM benchmarks has established that operationalising abstract phenomena into concrete tasks and metrics requires demonstrating that the resulting scores provide evidence for claims about the target phenomenon. A systematic review of 445 LLM benchmarks from leading conferences, conducted by 29 expert reviewers, found widespread patterns related to measured phenomena, tasks, and scoring metrics that undermine the validity of resulting claims. Freiesleben argues that the nomological account of construct validity, developed by Cronbach and Meehl, provides the most suitable foundation for current LLM capability research because it avoids the strong ontological commitments of the causal account while offering a more substantive framework than the inferential account.

Adaptive benchmarks intensify the construct-validity problem. The psychometric literature on measurement invariance has long recognized that adaptive testing threatens the comparability of scores. Recent work introduces ATLAS, an adaptive testing framework based on Item Response Theory (IRT) that estimates model ability using Fisher-information-guided item selection. Analysis of five major benchmarks reveals that 3–6% of items exhibit negative discrimination, indicating annotation errors that corrupt static evaluation. Among 4,000+ tested models, IRT ranks differ from accuracy ranks: models with the same accuracy get different IRT scores, and 23–31% of all models shift by more than 10 rank positions.

This paper develops a formal framework for this problem. The contribution is theoretical. No experiments are conducted, no simulations are executed, and no models are trained. The framework consists of (i) a set of formal definitions, (ii) a small number of proven propositions, (iii) borrowed theorems whose applicability to the LLM benchmark context is conditional, and (iv) proposed protocols and open conjectures. We are explicit throughout about which is which, and Section 7 provides a systematic epistemic audit.

We proceed in seven stages. Section 2 defines the measurement pipeline and the target construct. Section 3 formalizes the AMI problem and distinguishes three stabilities. Section 4 introduces the construct-sufficient representation via a decision-theoretic equivalence class. Section 5 formulates a falsifiable invariance-testing protocol. Section 6 extends the framework to Information-Adaptive Computation. Section 7 provides the epistemic audit and the research program.

