2. The Measurement Pipeline

2.1 Typed Formal Setup

Let  \mathcal{M}  denote a model class and  \mathcal{D}  a data space. A target construct is a latent property  C: \mathcal{M} \to \mathcal{P} , where  \mathcal{P}  is a performance space, typically  \mathbb{R}  or a partial order. Define:

·  D_t \in \Delta(\mathcal{D}) : data distribution at time  t 
·  \theta_t : benchmark-construction machinery (extraction model, prompts, filter, evaluator)
·  G_t = G(D_t, \theta_t): \Delta(\mathcal{D}) \to \mathcal{B} : generator to benchmark space
·  E: \mathcal{B} \times \mathcal{M} \to \mathbb{R} : evaluation procedure
·  S_t(m) = E(G(D_t, \theta_t), m) : observed score for model  m \in \mathcal{M} 

The measurement pipeline is  D_t \xrightarrow{G_t} B_t \xrightarrow{E} S_t .

2.2 Construct Validity via Nomological Networks

Following the nomological account of Cronbach and Meehl, a construct  C  is validly measured by  S  if  S  is embedded in a nomological network: a system of lawful relations between  C  and other constructs, and between  S  and other observable measures. Freiesleben argues that this framework is particularly suitable for LLM capability research because it articulates construct meaning through the web of lawful relations without requiring the strong ontological commitments of causal accounts.

Definition 2.1 (Nomological validity). Let  \mathcal{X}  be a set of external variables. Let  \mathcal{R}  be a set of lawful relations  R \subseteq \mathcal{P} \times \mathcal{X} . A score functional  S  nomologically measures  C  if there exists a set of relations  \mathcal{R}' \subseteq \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{X}  such that:

\forall R \in \mathcal{R}, \ \exists R' \in \mathcal{R}': \quad
(R \circ C) = (R' \circ S)

where  \circ  denotes relational composition. Equivalently,  S  reproduces the same pattern of lawful relations with external variables that  C  does.

Remark 2.1 (Status). Definition 2.1 is a definitional choice, not an empirical claim. It is strictly stronger than requiring a strictly monotone  \phi  with  \mathbb{E}[S] = \phi(C) + \varepsilon , because the monotone condition alone is nearly unfalsifiable: any score that is some increasing function of  C  qualifies, including one tracking a confound perfectly correlated with  C . The nomological condition requires  S  to participate in the same lawful web of relations, which is in principle testable against external correlates. However, we do not specify  \mathcal{R}  for any construct in this work. The framework provides a formal schema for testing construct invariance once  \mathcal{R}  is supplied. Constructing  \mathcal{R}  for a given capability remains an open problem (Problem 7.1).

2.3 Typed Equivalence Relations

We distinguish three equivalence relations:

·  \sim_C^{\text{data}}  on  \Delta(\mathcal{D}) :  D \sim_C^{\text{data}} D'  iff  D  and  D'  yield the same distribution of  C -relevant facts.
·  \sim_C^{\text{bench}}  on  \mathcal{B} :  B \sim_C^{\text{bench}} B'  iff for all  m, m' \in \mathcal{M} ,
  S_B(m) \geq S_B(m') \iff S_{B'}(m) \geq S_{B'}(m')
  and both orderings align with  C .
·  \sim_C^{\text{fact}}  on fact sets:  \mathcal{F} \sim_C^{\text{fact}} \mathcal{F}'  iff they support the same  C -relevant inferences.

The measurement pipeline is construct-invariant if  D_t \sim_C^{\text{data}} D_{t+1}  implies  G(D_t) \sim_C^{\text{bench}} G(D_{t+1}) .

Remark 2.2 (Status). These equivalence relations are stipulated, not discovered. Their value is judged by whether they enable useful theorems and testable protocols.

