4. Construct-Sufficient Representations

4.1 From Blackwell Sufficiency to Decision-Theoretic Equivalence

Blackwell's order compares two experiments via a garbling kernel. However, the Blackwell order is not transitive: sufficiency is not transitive. This means that even if  \mathcal{E}_t  is sufficient for  \mathcal{E}_{t+1} , and  \mathcal{E}_{t+1}  for  \mathcal{E}_{t+2} , it does not follow that  \mathcal{E}_t  is sufficient for  \mathcal{E}_{t+2} . For a framework concerned with regeneration over time, this is a critical flaw.

Definition 4.1 (Decision-theoretic equivalence). Let  \mathcal{D}_C  be a family of decision problems relevant to construct  C . Two experiments  \mathcal{E}_t = (D_t, G_t)  and  \mathcal{E}_{t+1} = (D_{t+1}, G_{t+1})  are construct-equivalent if they induce the same optimal decision rule for every decision problem in  \mathcal{D}_C :

\forall d \in \mathcal{D}_C: \quad \arg\max_{a} \mathbb{E}[U(a) \mid \mathcal{E}_t] = \arg\max_{a} \mathbb{E}[U(a) \mid \mathcal{E}_{t+1}]

where  U  is the utility function associated with  d .

Proposition 4.1. Decision-theoretic equivalence is transitive.

Proof. If  \mathcal{E}_t  and  \mathcal{E}_{t+1}  induce the same optimal decisions, and  \mathcal{E}_{t+1}  and  \mathcal{E}_{t+2}  induce the same optimal decisions, then by transitivity of equality,  \mathcal{E}_t  and  \mathcal{E}_{t+2}  induce the same optimal decisions.  \square 

Remark 4.1 (Status and limits). Proposition 4.1 is proven deductively. Decision-theoretic equivalence is weaker than Blackwell sufficiency (which requires a garbling kernel), but it is transitive and directly testable in principle: for a set of decision problems—model selection, capability thresholding, resource allocation—check whether the optimal actions coincide. However, we do not specify  \mathcal{D}_C  for any construct in this work. The choice of  \mathcal{D}_C  is a modelling decision; different choices yield different equivalence classes. Specifying  \mathcal{D}_C  for a given construct is an open problem (Problem 7.2).

4.2 Definition: Construct-Sufficient Representation

Definition 4.2 (Construct sufficiency).  \mathcal{E}_t  is construct-sufficient for  \mathcal{E}_{t+1}  if there exists a Markov kernel (garbling)  \gamma: \mathcal{B}_t \to \mathcal{B}_{t+1}  such that for every construct value  C \in \mathcal{C} :

\gamma \circ P_{\mathcal{E}_t}(\cdot \mid C) = P_{\mathcal{E}_{t+1}}(\cdot \mid C)

Equivalently,  \mathcal{E}_{t+1}  is a garbling of  \mathcal{E}_t . The construct-sufficient representation  T_C^\star  is the minimal such representation—the coarsest  \sigma -field that is sufficient for the family  \mathcal{P} = \{P_{\mathcal{E}_t}(\cdot \mid C)\}_{C \in \mathcal{C}} .

4.3 Existence and Uniqueness

Theorem 4.1 (Existence; Bahadur, 1957). If  \mathcal{P}  contains distributions on  \mathbb{R}^k  dominated by a  \sigma -finite measure, then a minimal sufficient  \sigma -field exists. Moreover, a complete sufficient statistic is minimal sufficient.

Remark 4.2 (Status and the domination assumption). Theorem 4.1 is a borrowed theorem, proven elsewhere. Its applicability to LLM benchmark data is conditional. High-dimensional discrete or mixed continuous-discrete response matrices typical of LLM benchmarks are frequently non-dominated. We therefore restrict the existence claim for  T_C^\star  to dominated sub-families and treat  T_C^\star  as a constructive surrogate approximated via Information Bottleneck or decodable IB methods for non-dominated regimes. Without this qualification, the representation risks being non-constructive for the very data regimes the framework targets. The approximation claim is a conjecture, not a proven equivalence (Problem 7.3).

Proposition 4.2 (Uniqueness). If  T  and  S  are both minimal sufficient statistics, then there exists a one-to-one measurable function  \psi  such that  T = \psi(S)  almost everywhere  \mathcal{P} .

Proof. Since  T  is minimal sufficient, for any sufficient statistic  S , there exists  \psi  with  T = \psi(S)  a.e.  \mathcal{P} . Since  S  is also minimal sufficient, there exists  \phi  with  S = \phi(T)  a.e.  \mathcal{P} . Composing,  T = \psi(\phi(T))  and  S = \phi(\psi(S))  a.e.  \mathcal{P} . Thus  \psi \circ \phi = \text{id}  and  \phi \circ \psi = \text{id} , establishing that  \psi  is a bijection.  \square 

4.4 Factorization Theorem for AMI

Proposition 4.3. Suppose  T_C^\star  is construct-sufficient for  \mathcal{P} . If:

1.  D_t \sim_C^{\text{data}} D_{t+1}  (data construct-invariance),
2.  G_1  is  C -faithful:  T_C^\star(D) = T_C^\star(D') \implies T_C^\star(G_1(D)) = T_C^\star(G_1(D')) ,
3.  G_2  is  C -equivariant:  G_2  maps  C -equivalent fact sets to  C -equivalent benchmark structures,
4.  E  is  C -valid: the evaluator orders benchmarks consistently with the nomological network of  C ,

then regeneration preserves  C .

Proof. The proof proceeds by chasing the commutative diagram:

\begin{array}{ccc}
\mathcal{D} & \xrightarrow{G_1} & \mathcal{F} & \xrightarrow{G_2} & \mathcal{B} \\
\downarrow T_C^\star & & & & \downarrow \pi_C \\
\mathcal{T} & \xrightarrow{G_1'} & \mathcal{F}_C & \xrightarrow{G_2'} & \mathcal{B}_C
\end{array}

Step 1. By condition (1),  T_C^\star(D_t) = T_C^\star(D_{t+1})  a.e.  \mathcal{P} .

Step 2. By condition (2),  T_C^\star(G_1(D_t)) = T_C^\star(G_1(D_{t+1}))  a.e.  \mathcal{P} .

Step 3. By condition (3),  G_2  maps  C -equivalent fact sets to  C -equivalent benchmarks:  G_2(G_1(D_t)) \sim_C^{\text{bench}} G_2(G_1(D_{t+1})) .

Step 4. By condition (4), for all  m, m' \in \mathcal{M} ,  S_{B_t}(m) \geq S_{B_t}(m') \iff S_{B_{t+1}}(m) \geq S_{B_{t+1}}(m') , with both orderings aligned with  C .  \square 

Remark 4.3 (Status and the observable–latent gap). Proposition 4.3 is proven conditionally: if conditions (1)–(4) hold, then regeneration preserves  C . The conditions themselves are not verified empirically in this work. Condition (2) can in principle be made testable without access to the latent  T_C^\star . If stratified extraction recall for every  C -relevant fact type exceeds a pre-specified threshold  \rho , and the rank correlation of fact-type recall profiles across regenerations exceeds  \tau , then  G_1  is  C -faithful for the observable fact space. However, this criterion certifies faithfulness only with respect to the observable fact space. Systematic reviews of construct validity in LLM benchmarks repeatedly show that high surface-level fidelity can coexist with construct drift (e.g., when items become saturated or when confounds shift). The gap between observable-fact fidelity and latent-construct fidelity should be stated explicitly and quantified via external nomological correlations. The claim that observable recall thresholds imply latent faithfulness is a conjecture, not a theorem.

