6. Information-Adaptive Computation

6.1 Formal Bridge from Benchmark Construction to Online Agency

Let the system state at time  t  be the pair  s_t = (D_t, \theta_t) , where  D_t  is the current data distribution and  \theta_t  is the current construction machinery. An information-acquisition action  a  is a choice of extraction, filtering, or generation operation:

a \in \mathcal{A} = \mathcal{A}_{\text{extract}} \times \mathcal{A}_{\text{filter}} \times \mathcal{A}_{\text{generate}}

The information state after action  a  is  S_a = (D_t^a, \theta_t^a) , the updated data distribution and machinery after applying  a .

6.2 Value of Computation and Construct Sufficiency

Let  V_C  be the expected preservation of construct-relevant inference. The objective is:

a^\star = \arg\max_a \left[ \mathbb{E}[\Delta V_C(a \mid s_t)] - \lambda C(a) \right]

where  C(a)  is cost and  \lambda  is cost sensitivity.

6.3 Monotone Relation Between VOC and Construct-Sufficient Information

Proposition 6.1. Under the assumption that the utility function is proper and the construct-sufficient representation  T_C^\star  is decision-theoretically sufficient, the expected value of computation is non-decreasing in the construct-sufficient information gain:

\mathbb{E}[\Delta V_C(a \mid s_t)] \geq 0 \quad \text{whenever} \quad \mathbb{E}[\Delta I(T_C^\star(D); S_a)] \geq 0

Proof sketch. By decision-theoretic equivalence, if  T_C^\star  is sufficient for the decision problem family, any decision rule based on  S_a  achieves risk bounded by the information content  I(T_C^\star(D); S_a) . The data processing inequality gives monotonicity: more information cannot decrease the achievable expected utility.  \square 

Remark 6.1 (Status and open direction). Proposition 6.1 is proven conditionally on the stated assumptions. A first-order linear relation  \mathbb{E}[\Delta V_C] = \gamma \cdot \mathbb{E}[\Delta I] + o(\|\Delta I\|)  requires additional assumptions—either a small-signal (Fisher-information) argument or a specific parametric utility family. The metareasoning literature already struggles with exact VOC calculations, and the smoothness or parametric utility assumptions needed for a linear relation are not yet supplied. We therefore reframe this section as an open research direction rather than a completed formal bridge. Identifying the utility families (threshold-based, concave) under which stronger results hold remains a priority (Problem 7.4).

6.4 IAC Objective

a^\star = \arg\max_a \left[ \mathbb{E}[\Delta I(T_C^\star(D); S_a)] - \lambda C(a) \right]

This connects to rational metareasoning for LLMs, where training with a VOC-based reward function reduces inference costs without sacrificing performance. The connection is conceptual; no experiments are conducted here.

