7. Epistemic Audit and Research Program

7.1 Epistemic Status of Claims

A theoretical contribution must be scrupulous about what it has and has not established. The framework contains five distinct types of claims.

Proven deductive results. Propositions 3.1, 3.2, 4.1, 4.2, and the conditional factorization theorem (Proposition 4.3) are mathematically established within the stated assumptions. Proposition 6.1 is proven conditionally. These results do not depend on empirical data and would remain true even if no LLM benchmark ever existed.

Definitional choices. Definitions 2.1, 2.3, 3.1–3.3, 4.1, and 4.2 are stipulated formalisations. They cannot be true or false; they can only be more or less useful. Their value is judged by whether they enable useful theorems and testable protocols.

Borrowed theorems. Bahadur's existence theorem, Meredith's invariance hierarchy, the WLSMV estimation theory, alignment optimization, and the TOST procedure are proven or established elsewhere. Their applicability to the LLM benchmark context is conditional and, in several cases, conjectural.

Conjectures and proposed protocols. The invariance-testing protocol of Section 5, the AO/BAMI recommendation for  N < 200 , the minimum  N \geq 150 , the observable faithfulness criterion (Remark 4.3), the TOST margin derivation (Proposal 5.2), and the validity vector  \mathbf{V}_B  are proposed but not validated. They are the empirical frontiers of the framework.

Speculative extensions. The IAC linear relation, the existence of  T_C^\star  for real LLM benchmark data under non-domination, the specification of  \mathcal{R}  and  \mathcal{D}_C , and the claim that the framework "detects failures missed by aggregate metrics" are speculative. They go beyond what the formalism or the borrowed literature licenses. They are stated as open problems, not as results.

7.2 What the Framework Does Not Claim

The framework does not claim that any experiment was run, any simulation was executed, or any model was trained. The Monte-Carlo design that appeared in earlier versions of this work is not a result; it is a proposed test. The predicted results under that design are hypotheses, not findings. The framework's central practical claim—that construct drift can occur while difficulty and ranking stability hold—is a logical possibility established by Proposition 3.2, not an empirical discovery. Whether this possibility is realised in any particular benchmark is an empirical question that this work does not answer.

7.3 The Research Program

Three parallel invariance problems.

Problem Equivalence Relation Object Preserved Structure
Semantic invariance  \sim_{\text{semantic}}  Representations Meaning
Construct invariance  \sim_C^{\text{bench}}  Benchmarks Nomological network
Information sufficiency Decision-theoretic equivalence Experiments Decision-theoretic value

Validity as a vector.

\mathbf{V}_B = (V_C, V_T, V_E, V_R)

·  V_C : construct validity (nomological network embedding)
·  V_T : transformation fidelity (extraction recall by fact type)
·  V_E : evaluation validity (IRT item diagnostics with assumption-checking)
·  V_R : regeneration invariance (measurement invariance via AO/BAMI)

These should not be collapsed into a single scalar without an explicit theory of composition. The vector is a conceptual schema; its operationalisation is an open problem.

7.4 Open Problems (Reordered by Priority)

Problem 7.1 (Specification of the nomological network). For a given construct  C , how is the set  \mathcal{R}  of lawful relations elicited, validated, and stabilised? The framework presupposes  \mathcal{R}  but does not supply it.

Problem 7.2 (Specification of the decision problem family). For a given construct  C , what is the canonical  \mathcal{D}_C ? Different choices yield different equivalence classes. Is there a principled way to select  \mathcal{D}_C ?

Problem 7.3 (Constructive approximation of  T_C^\star ). For non-dominated families, can  T_C^\star  be approximated via decodable Information Bottleneck? What guarantees exist that the approximation is sufficient for the decision problems in  \mathcal{D}_C ?

Problem 7.4 (Invariance testing with small  N ). What is the power of AO/BAMI under realistic LLM benchmark sample sizes ( N < 100 )? Existing simulations suggest  N \geq 150  is required for reliable factor means, but the performance for variances remains uncertain.

Problem 7.5 (Linear value of computation). What additional smoothness or parametric utility assumptions yield a first-order linear relation between construct-sufficient information gain and value of computation?

Problem 7.6 (Online monitoring). Can the validity vector  \mathbf{V}_B  be monitored online using IRT diagnostics across repeated regeneration?

7.5 Positioning Relative to Concurrent Work

This framework is the formal invariance-under-regeneration layer that several empirical and philosophical contributions currently lack. ATLAS provides the adaptive IRT selection mechanism and documents rank divergences at scale. The 445-benchmark systematic review provides the empirical motivation by documenting widespread under-specification of target phenomena. Freiesleben provides the philosophical foundation by arguing for nomological networks as the most suitable framework for LLM capability research. The AO/BAMI simulation literature provides the methodological tools for small-sample invariance testing. This paper integrates these strands into a unified formal framework for construct-sufficient benchmark generation. The integration is theoretical; the empirical validation of the proposed protocols remains to be done.

---

8. Conclusion

A benchmark should be evaluated not only by the systems it measures, but also by the invariants preserved by the process that constructs it. Adaptive benchmarks introduce a new class of validity threats: regeneration may preserve aggregate difficulty while drifting in construct. The framework of Adaptive Measurement Validity formalizes this problem and connects it to four established research streams:

1. Construct validity via nomological networks, formalized as reproduction of lawful relations with external variables rather than an unconstrained monotone transformation;
2. Decision-theoretic equivalence replacing the non-transitive Blackwell order, providing a transitive and directly testable criterion for construct sufficiency;
3. Measurement invariance via alignment optimization and Bayesian approximate invariance for small-sample settings, with explicit IRT assumption-checking as a prerequisite and strict invariance required for dichotomous-item factor mean comparisons;
4. Equivalence testing with nomologically derived margins, replacing arbitrary thresholds with principled criteria grounded in the smallest effect size of interest.

The framework's formal results are proven deductively. Its protocols are proposed and remain to be validated. Its speculative extensions are labelled as open problems. The existence of  T_C^\star  is restricted to dominated families, with constructive surrogates required for non-dominated regimes. The gap between observable-fact fidelity and latent-construct fidelity is stated explicitly. The necessity of scalar invariance is scoped to the particular decision problems under consideration. The nomological network  \mathcal{R}  and the decision problem family  \mathcal{D}_C  are parameters of the framework, not supplied by it.

A purely theoretical PhD is not weaker for lacking empirical validation. It is different. Its contribution is the formal framework—the definitions, the proven propositions, the conceptual distinctions. These are the load-bearing elements. The speculative elements are not flaws; they are the frontier. The strongest theoretical thesis is one that says: Here is what I have proven. Here is what I have defined. Here is what I have borrowed. Here is what I conjecture. Here is what remains open. That is a contribution to knowledge.


