A.4 Bahadur’s Theorem: Existence of Minimal Sufficient Statistics

Theorem A.1 (Bahadur, 1957). Let  \mathcal{P} = \{P_\theta : \theta \in \Theta\}  be a family of probability measures on  (\mathbb{R}^k, \mathcal{B}^k)  dominated by a  \sigma -finite measure  \mu . Then there exists a minimal sufficient statistic  T: \mathbb{R}^k \to \mathbb{R}^m  for  \mathcal{P} .

Proof sketch. The proof proceeds in three steps.

Step 1: Construction of a candidate statistic. For each  x \in \mathbb{R}^k , define the likelihood function  L_x: \Theta \to [0, \infty)  by  L_x(\theta) = dP_\theta/d\mu(x) . Define the equivalence relation  x \sim x'  iff  L_x(\theta) = c(x, x') L_{x'}(\theta)  for all  \theta \in \Theta , where  c(x, x') > 0 . Let  T(x)  be the equivalence class of  x  under this relation.

Step 2: Sufficiency of T. The factorization theorem for dominated families states that  T  is sufficient iff there exist measurable functions  g_\theta  and  h  such that:

\frac{dP_\theta}{d\mu}(x) = g_\theta(T(x)) \cdot h(x)

for all  \theta \in \Theta . Since  T(x)  encodes the likelihood function up to proportionality, and the proportionality constant  c(x, x')  depends only on  x, x'  (not  \theta ), the factorization holds with  g_\theta(T(x)) = L_x(\theta)  and  h(x) = 1 .

Step 3: Minimality of T. Let  S  be any sufficient statistic for  \mathcal{P} . By the factorization theorem, there exist  g'_\theta  and  h'  such that  dP_\theta/d\mu(x) = g'_\theta(S(x)) \cdot h'(x) . Since  T(x)  encodes the likelihood function, and the likelihood function is determined by  S(x)  through  g'_\theta(S(x)) , there exists a measurable function  \phi  such that  T(x) = \phi(S(x))  almost everywhere  \mathcal{P} . Thus  T  is a function of every sufficient statistic, establishing minimal sufficiency.

The result is established under the mild restriction that the sample space is, or may be taken to be, a subset of the  m -dimensional Cartesian space  (1 \leq m \leq \infty) , and that the alternative distributions of the sample point admit density functions with respect to a fixed  \sigma -finite measure . Minimal sufficient statistics exist under weak assumptions:  \mathcal{P}  contains distributions on  \mathcal{R}^k  dominated by a  \sigma -finite measure .  \square 

Remark A.4 (Non-dominated families). The domination assumption is essential. For non-dominated families, minimal sufficient statistics may fail to exist. In such cases, the construct-sufficient representation  T_C^\star  must be treated as a constructive surrogate approximated via Information Bottleneck methods. This is an open problem (Problem 7.3 in the main text).

---

A.5 Uniqueness of Minimal Sufficient Statistics

Proposition A.4. If  T  and  S  are both minimal sufficient statistics for  \mathcal{P} , then there exists a one-to-one measurable function  \psi  such that  T = \psi(S)  almost everywhere  \mathcal{P} .

Proof. Since  T  is minimal sufficient, for any sufficient statistic  S , there exists  \psi  with  T = \psi(S)  a.e.  \mathcal{P} . Since  S  is also minimal sufficient, there exists  \phi  with  S = \phi(T)  a.e.  \mathcal{P} . Composing,  T = \psi(\phi(T))  and  S = \phi(\psi(S))  a.e.  \mathcal{P} . Thus  \psi \circ \phi = \text{id}  and  \phi \circ \psi = \text{id} , establishing that  \psi  is a bijection.  \square 

Remark A.5. The uniqueness result guarantees that “the”  T_C^\star  is well-defined only up to one-to-one measurable transformation. Different choices of  T_C^\star  are equivalent for the purpose of measuring  C , since any decision rule based on one can be transformed into a decision rule based on the other without loss of information.

