A.6 Factorization Theorem for AMI

Proposition A.5. Suppose  T_C^\star  is construct-sufficient for  \mathcal{P} . If:

1.  D_t \sim_C^{\text{data}} D_{t+1}  (data construct-invariance),
2.  G_1  is  C -faithful:  T_C^\star(D) = T_C^\star(D') \implies T_C^\star(G_1(D)) = T_C^\star(G_1(D')) ,
3.  G_2  is  C -equivariant:  G_2  maps  C -equivalent fact sets to  C -equivalent benchmark structures,
4.  E  is  C -valid: the evaluator orders benchmarks consistently with the nomological network of  C ,

then regeneration preserves  C .

Proof. The proof proceeds by chasing the commutative diagram:

\begin{array}{ccc}
\mathcal{D} & \xrightarrow{G_1} & \mathcal{F} & \xrightarrow{G_2} & \mathcal{B} \\
\downarrow T_C^\star & & & & \downarrow \pi_C \\
\mathcal{T} & \xrightarrow{G_1'} & \mathcal{F}_C & \xrightarrow{G_2'} & \mathcal{B}_C
\end{array}

Step 1. By condition (1),  T_C^\star(D_t) = T_C^\star(D_{t+1})  a.e.  \mathcal{P} .

Step 2. By condition (2),  T_C^\star(G_1(D_t)) = T_C^\star(G_1(D_{t+1}))  a.e.  \mathcal{P} . This follows directly from the definition of  C -faithfulness: since  T_C^\star(D_t) = T_C^\star(D_{t+1}) , the  C -relevant content of the extracted fact sets is identical.

Step 3. By condition (3),  G_2  maps  C -equivalent fact sets to  C -equivalent benchmark structures. Since  G_1(D_t)  and  G_1(D_{t+1})  are  C -equivalent (from Step 2), their  G_2 -images are  C -equivalent benchmarks:  G_2(G_1(D_t)) \sim_C^{\text{bench}} G_2(G_1(D_{t+1})) .

Step 4. By condition (4), the evaluator  E  preserves this equivalence in the ordering of scores. Specifically, for all  m, m' \in \mathcal{M} :

S_{B_t}(m) \geq S_{B_t}(m') \iff S_{B_{t+1}}(m) \geq S_{B_{t+1}}(m')

and both orderings align with  C . This establishes  G(D_t, \theta_t) \sim_C^{\text{bench}} G(D_{t+1}, \theta_{t+1}) , completing the proof.  \square 

Remark A.6 (Testability of conditions). Conditions (1)–(3) are in principle testable via stratified extraction recall and measurement invariance analysis. Condition (4) reduces to the LLM-as-judge validation problem, for which IRT diagnostics with assumption-checking provide tools (Appendix C).

