Appendix B: Measurement Invariance Protocol Details

B.1 Meredith’s Invariance Hierarchy

Meredith (1993) defined a hierarchy of levels of invariance that validate different types of between-group comparisons on latent variables . The seminal article suggested a hierarchical series of measurement invariance tests such that models with the fewest constraints are tested first and more constraints are sequentially imposed . Later papers have generally deemed the four hierarchical steps as (a) configural, (b) metric, (c) scalar, and (d) strict .

Configural invariance assesses whether the factor structure and pattern are the same between groups. Metric invariance assesses whether the loadings are equal across groups; if upheld, it implies that any group differences in the item covariances are attributable to group differences in the latent variable(s). Scalar invariance assesses whether the loadings and item intercepts are equal across groups; if retained, this implies that any group differences in the item means and item covariances are due to group differences in latent variable(s). Scalar invariance is considered sufficient to meaningfully compare observed or latent variable scores. Strict invariance assesses whether the loadings, item intercepts, and item residual variances are equal across groups .

B.2 Alignment Optimization

Alignment optimization (AO) is a method for estimating approximate measurement invariance without the need for exact scalar invariance. Instead of constraining all parameters to be equal across groups, AO estimates a set of cross-cluster measurement parameters (factor loadings and intercepts) that are allowed to vary, with a loss function that penalizes large differences from the group-specific means.

The alignment procedure searches means  \alpha_{g0}  and standard deviations  \psi_{g0}  using an alignment optimization function  F  . Muthén and Asparouhov (2014) proposed to use a component loss function (CLF)  f  from EFA rotations:

f = \sqrt[4]{\chi^2 + \epsilon}

where  \epsilon  is a small positive constant . The alignment loss function is combined with the rotation loss function to estimate group-specific factor means and variances without requiring exact measurement invariance .

Sample size requirements. Simulation evidence indicates that a sample size of  N = 100  is too small to produce reliable estimates in the alignment approach . The minimal group size required for alignment was 250 when the number of groups was three; the minimal group size was 150 when the number of groups was nine . According to Muthén and Asparouhov (2018), correlations between estimated and actual factor means and variances should preferably not be below .98 and never below .95 for the alignment method to be reliable. Simulations with  N = 150, 200,  and  250  showed that correlations for factor means were above the cutoff in all simulations, but for factor variances with lower sample sizes, some correlations fell below .95, though still above .90 .

B.3 WLSMV Estimation for Ordinal Indicators

For ordinal indicators, the weighted least squares mean-and-variance-adjusted (WLSMV) estimator is appropriate. The model is:

X_{ij}^* = \nu_j + \lambda_j \eta_i + \epsilon_{ij}

with observed ordinal response generated by thresholding:

X_{ij} = k \iff \tau_{j,k-1} < X_{ij}^* \leq \tau_{j,k}

In theta parameterization, the residual variance is fixed to 1 and the latent variance is free, which is appropriate for invariance research . For WLSMV estimation, recommended fit index values are CFI  \geq 0.95  and RMSEA  \leq 0.05 . The SRMR is not recommended with the WLSMV estimator . Monte Carlo evidence supports  \Delta RMSEA over  \Delta CFI for ordinal data .

The invariance hierarchy for ordinal indicators is:

Level Constraint Test Statistic
Configural Same factor structure RMSEA  \leq .05 , CFI  \geq .95 
Metric Equal loadings  \Delta RMSEA  < +.015 
Scalar Equal loadings and thresholds  \Delta RMSEA  < +.015 
Strict Equal residual variances  \Delta\chi^2  (with caution)

