Appendix C: IRT Diagnostics and Assumption Checking

C.1 IRT Model Specification

For dichotomous items, the two-parameter logistic (2PL) model is:

P(X_{ij} = 1 \mid \theta_i, \alpha_j, \beta_j) = \frac{1}{1 + \exp(-\alpha_j(\theta_i - \beta_j))}

where  \theta_i  is the latent ability of model  i ,  \alpha_j  is the discrimination parameter of item  j , and  \beta_j  is the difficulty parameter.

The three-parameter logistic (3PL) model adds a guessing parameter  c_j :

P(X_{ij} = 1 \mid \theta_i, \alpha_j, \beta_j, c_j) = c_j + \frac{1 - c_j}{1 + \exp(-\alpha_j(\theta_i - \beta_j))}

C.2 Assumption Checking

Before any invariance testing, the IRT model must be validated. The core assumptions are:

1. Unidimensionality: the items have one underlying latent dimension.
2. Local independence: the items are uncorrelated when controlling for the latent dimension.
3. Monotonicity: the probability of endorsing a higher level on an item increases as the person’s trait level increases .

Unidimensionality. Conduct exploratory factor analysis (EFA) on the polychoric correlation matrix. Retain factors with eigenvalues > 1.

Local Independence. Compute the local dependence statistic:

LD\text{-}X^2_{jj'} = \sum_{k=1}^{K} \frac{(O_{jj'k} - E_{jj'k})^2}{E_{jj'k}}

where  O_{jj'k}  is the observed frequency of response pattern  k  and  E_{jj'k}  is the expected frequency under local independence. Values exceeding 10 indicate substantial local dependence .

Monotonicity. Fit a non-parametric IRT model (e.g., Mokken scale analysis) and test whether the item response function is non-decreasing. Monotonicity is evaluated through inspection of the item-category characteristic curves . Violations indicate that examinees with lower proficiency levels may receive higher scores.

Normality of Latent Ability Distribution. Use posterior predictive checks. Departures from normality (skewness > 2, kurtosis > 7) indicate violation.

C.3 Differential Item Functioning (DIF) Testing

DIF occurs when item parameters differ across regenerations. The null hypothesis is:

H_0: \alpha_j^{(t)} = \alpha_j^{(t+1)}, \quad \beta_j^{(t)} = \beta_j^{(t+1)}

for anchor items. The test statistic is:

\text{DIF}_{j} = \frac{(\hat{\beta}_j^{(t)} - \hat{\beta}_j^{(t+1)})^2}{\text{Var}(\hat{\beta}_j^{(t)}) + \text{Var}(\hat{\beta}_j^{(t+1)})}

Under  H_0 , DIF_j \sim \chi^2_1 . Reject  H_0  at level  \alpha  if DIF_j > \chi^2_{1,1-\alpha} . A test item is typically considered free of DIF if its item response function is the same across groups .

